Use carbonate chemistry to connect gas, acid/base, and mineral equilibria (@gc-carbonate: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../sources/Henry-Law-Sander.lingua#r_4HW63C2ZE42PFRFC294FDPTYWH", #source "../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T", #general-chemistry "../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/numerics/A-numerical-validation-study.lingua#r_7EY9S6DXHFHSJAGA30W35A4ZD8", #water-and-sanitation "../../../utilities/water-and-sanitation/foundations/Alkalinity-hardness-buffering-and-carbonate-boundaries.lingua#r_59GA6HCRGGMESSYPMGFW2T7BE0") { r_0WTZ0V8V2K18X7XJZQFAQ4KB07
**Read:** [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the complete geochemical-carbonate application and exact acid/base calculation lessons; [Compilation of Henry’s law constants, version 5.0.0 — Rolf Sander](../sources/Henry-Law-Sander.lingua#r_4HW63C2ZE42PFRFC294FDPTYWH), the convention/temperature discussion and selected relevant sources; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), systematic multicomponent equilibrium.

An aqueous carbonate model connects dissolved inorganic carbon species, protonation, gas partition, charge balance, and possibly mineral phases. Define the species convention for dissolved CO₂/carbonic acid, the constants and temperature, analytical totals, other ions, and the gas/solid boundary conditions before solving.

The diprotic distribution fractions provide one useful component account. For a defined carbonate-only alkalinity model, total alkalinity can be expressed as [HCO₃⁻] + 2[CO₃²⁻] + [OH⁻] − [H⁺], with its reference convention. Real waters can require additional components and operational measurement interpretation. State what is omitted.

An open system with an imposed CO₂ partial pressure has a different carbon constraint from a sealed system with fixed total carbon. Henry's law applies to its specified dissolved species and convention; total inorganic carbon includes subsequent equilibria. Adding a carbonate mineral introduces phase availability and a solubility relation rather than an unlimited unspecified source of carbon and metal.

**Work:** build one explicit closed-water model and one open-gas model using identified constants and unit conventions. Compare the unknown/equation sets before computing. Trace how pH, free carbonate, and a mineral-saturation prediction respond to changed total carbon, alkalinity, or gas pressure. Identify what an actual sample and measurement procedure would need to establish.

Read the complete geochemical-carbonate application in Chem1 and the Henry convention material. This is a demanding integrative example rather than a substitute for later geochemistry or water engineering. Follow [Build a numerical validation argument across several kinds of evidence](../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/numerics/A-numerical-validation-study.lingua#r_7EY9S6DXHFHSJAGA30W35A4ZD8) before the original numerical equilibrium implementation, using its general-chemistry continuation for the next step.

**Continue:** [Build a numerical validation argument across several kinds of evidence](../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/numerics/A-numerical-validation-study.lingua#r_7EY9S6DXHFHSJAGA30W35A4ZD8).

**Return:** [General chemistry](../General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V).

